--TEST--
`Core\IO::walk` takes the whole tree when it is called — the value it answers may be walked more than once and does not notice a file written afterwards — and a directory is an entry as well as somewhere to descend, so an empty one walks to nothing and a directory holding one walks to exactly one — spec § 14, ADR 0053 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound on both sides first: a directory with nothing in it and a directory
// whose only content is an empty directory are one entry apart. A member that
// reported files and descended silently past directories answers 0 to both, and
// the first line alone would not have caught it.
Core\IO::makeDir("hollow");
Core\IO::makeDir("outer/inner");

int $hollow = 0;
foreach (Core\IO::walk("hollow") as string $entry) {
    $hollow = $hollow + 1;
}

int $outer = 0;
string $only = "";
foreach (Core\IO::walk("outer") as string $entry) {
    $outer = $outer + 1;
    $only = Core\Str::replace($entry, Core\Path::SEPARATOR, "/");
}

echo "hollow: ", $hollow as string, ", outer: ", $outer as string, " (", $only, ")\n";

// `Iterable<string>` is a value a `foreach` asks for a walk, not a walk that has
// already begun: two loops over one value see the same tree. A value that were
// the walk itself would answer nothing the second time, which is exactly the
// mistake PHP's own generators make available.
Core\IO\Walk $held = Core\IO::walk("tree");

int $first = 0;
foreach ($held as string $entry) {
    $first = $first + 1;
}

int $second = 0;
foreach ($held as string $entry) {
    $second = $second + 1;
}

echo "first walk: ", $first as string, ", second walk: ", $second as string, "\n";

// And the decision `Core\IO\Walk`'s own docs argue: the tree is read when the
// member is called, so the value is a snapshot rather than a live view. A file
// written after it was built is not in it, and is in the next one -- the two
// counts named together, because either alone reads as a member that simply
// answered a number.
Core\IO::write("tree/late.txt", "late");

int $again = 0;
foreach ($held as string $entry) {
    $again = $again + 1;
}

int $fresh = 0;
foreach (Core\IO::walk("tree") as string $entry) {
    $fresh = $fresh + 1;
}

echo "the value already taken: ", $again as string, ", a walk taken now: ", $fresh as string, "\n";
--FILE tree/one.txt--
one
--FILE tree/two.txt--
two
--FILE tree/nested/three.txt--
three
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
hollow: 0, outer: 1 (inner)
first walk: 4, second walk: 4
the value already taken: 4, a walk taken now: 5
